    "compose_patient": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You came in because you had dark, tarry stools for 2 days, one episode of vomiting blood, and dizziness.\n\nThe team found you had a bleeding ulcer in the first part of your small bowel (a duodenal ulcer). A test also showed you have a stomach bug called H. pylori."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team did the following:\n\n- A camera test (OGD) on Day 1. The doctor injected medicine into the ulcer and put a small clip on it to stop the bleeding.\n- You were given 2 units of blood.\n- You were given a drip to protect your stomach (an intravenous PPI).\n- A blood thinner (apixaban) that you take regularly was stopped during your stay.\n- A test confirmed the H. pylori bug was present."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "The following are planned after you leave hospital:\n\n- A gastroenterology clinic appointment and a repeat camera test (OGD) at 6–8 weeks to check the ulcer has healed.\n- Your GP will check your blood count (Hb).\n- Your GP will confirm that the H. pylori bug has been cleared.\n- Your GP will make a decision about restarting your blood thinner (apixaban)."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.4",
          "heading": "safety_net",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{SAFETY_NET}}"
        }
      ],
